A||N||13||JeffDm||'88 Model & Winter Drivability?||||5||
Z||000000||JeffDm||12-11-2000||12:18 PM||||I may soon be an owner of an '88 Fiero.  Nothing too special, it "just" has the 4 cylinder engine but I feel I got a good deal.<P>This thing is comming from a more temperate state in the US, and I will be primarily driving it in Michigan's wonderful lake effect weather, snow in the winter and rain in the summer (not that much rain but we get a few very strong storms).<P>I have done extremely well with two major FWD systems, the '84 Laser / Daytona platform and a '85 Grand Am.  How much different is the Fiero mid engine / rear drive arrangement in terms of rough weather drivability?||||reg||5
Z||000001||retrostudd||12-11-2000||01:02 PM||||I havent driven my fiero too much as of yet because im working on restoration first. But i will tell you this much. Even with a rear wheel drive that has the weight of the engine over the wheels it is still much harder to drive in the winter then that of a front wheel drive car.  Right now i drive an 87 reagal( rear wheell drive) and its a whitch in the snow, your power isnt in your directional control so it gets a little fun at times.  As long as your a sound and careful driver you should be fine, just be careful out there.||||reg||12
Z||000002||onfire||12-11-2000||01:21 PM||||If it has good tires, then there should be no problem. I used to drive my 85 gt in the snow all the time it did great. Never got stuck or spun out. It had surprisingly good traction.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/files/SNOW.JPG">  <P>------------------<BR>"I seen it on the internet, and I believe everthing on the net."<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank>Check out my waist of banbwidth.</A><p>[This message has been edited by onfire (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||pHoOl||12-11-2000||01:32 PM||||I'm driving on the lake effect snow right now.  Well okay, not right now, but the last couple of days.  In my 88GT, i put 100lbs of sand in the front.  My front end still slides around when i try to stop and turn at the same time, but that just might be my bald tires on the front, and the brakes were messed up until this weekend.  <P>However... go to a big parking lot on the weekend, and practice how it feels when you lose traction.  Everybody likes fwd cars in the snow cause they think that you lose traction less.  personally (still a young driver), i've had it both ways, and i like having rear wheel drive.  <P>I know i'll get flamed for saying this, but it seems a lot easier to control a rwd car in the snow than a fwd.  Maybe it's cause i'm practiced at it, but with fwd, if you lose traction with your drive wheels, you also lose steering.  <P>anyway, i went on a bit of a tangent.  I think with the 4 banger you should be okay.  I lose the back wheels when i just barely lean into the gas, but mines a V6.  Just be carefull, and practice.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||12-11-2000||01:59 PM||||With above average tread on the tires, you should have no problems.<BR>pHoOl hit right with the FWD cars, you loose traction in the front you loose steering as well. The same thing goes with RWD cars only it happens different. When you loose traction, you loose the ass end! It will (and does) pass you (either side) if the correct action isn't taken. I used to (when driving one in winter) put 2 80lb. bags of salt in the rear to have additional weight for traction, never bothered with the front. Just remember when you loose traction, take foot off gas, steer in the direction of the skid or where you want to go if it's in that direction and let it strighten out and stabilize before reapplying gas (or shift to netural/depress clutch if manual, if you don't think you can recover, espically on ICE) Plan ahead, leave yourself a way out, don't follow too closely, drive the conditions, don't be in a hurry and don't do anything stupid!<BR>Finding a place to practice is a good idea, just make sure there is nothing there to cause any damage to you or others.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JeffDm||12-11-2000||02:18 PM||||Thanks for the comments.  It sounds like the Fiero is extremely light on the front end, which is not surprising but apparently it affects handling more than I thought it did.<P>It will by my first mid engine / rear drive car.<P>I am not sure what I will do with the car yet, I think I might let it set until the worst of the winter is over, when I renew my insurance I'll switch cars then, that's a couple months from now, that way I can get used to the dynamics without the excessive snow...<P>Personally, I very strongly prefer front/engine front drive over FE/RD or even FE/4WD setups, but I did get a lot of experience on FE/FD, nearly 9 years of it now.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||12-11-2000||05:24 PM||||I have had my Fiero out in the snow twice... both just on my street to see how it would react.  The combination of my performance tires, their wide tread and V6 engine leave it sliding all over.  But with that said my dad has been driving his 87 Coupe (4 cyl) every winter for the past 10 years without a problem.  It cuts thru the snow with very few problems.  Although it will fishtail a little, the throttle is easy enough to control to prevent it from becoming a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||12-11-2000||06:11 PM||||I heard that 14" wheels are better in snow.  If I could afford it I would get a coupe for the winter because I have had some scary experiences in my GT when dealing with bad weather conditions.  Now I just drive very very very carefully and slow in bad weather, so far so good<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tryxalon||12-11-2000||08:37 PM||||The best car I have ever had in the snow was a FIAT X1/9. I have travelled in snow that was 28" deep. Yes, the car is 46" tall.  <P>It shares with our Fieros an underbody that has a "skid pad" effect and a weight low enough to ride ON the snow. <P>The weight of the engine is already over the wheels and that should give excellent traction. My biggest problem is the 50 series "performance rated" tires I have are both a little worn and  not "aggressive enough in the tread" to shed the snow rapidly. <P> I live in Grand Rapids MI and this is my first Fiero, but I had no problems today, even though we got "wheel hub" deep snow before I left work. <P>As others have said: Good tires, patience, control, and common sense will get you through just about anything. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||12-11-2000||10:16 PM||||Right now Im driving a Volvo 240DL wagon as my winter car, rwd, and a heavy thing at that, but with this snow we've been getting here in mid-michigan, I've had a few moments where I almost didnt make turns.<BR>    Right now my fiero is in storage, with many of the parts in my basement for restoration over the winter.  My uncle told me when he gave me the car that when you drive it in a lot of snow, it can build up underneath it and lift the car up.  I guess if you add weight both front and back, you could get away with it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||pHoOl||12-11-2000||11:13 PM||||I'm in grand rapids area too, wanna cruise sometime when this "winter snow advisory" is over?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||N_Man||12-12-2000||02:17 AM||||two words: Snow Tires<BR>they will make all the difference in the world.  Doesnt matter what you drive, how many wheels get power, or where the engine is, if you have the right tires life is good.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||05:54 PM||||Hats off to you guys who deal with actual weather and seasons!<P>Down here in Sunny, Shakey, Smoggy, Southern California we have to deal with 70 degree winters and occasional rain! (The rain frightens most Southern Californians because we all seem to drive into each other in it...)<P>Thought I would try to share some of California sunshine with yuz guys!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000013||Johnson's85SE||12-12-2000||06:17 PM||||Hello, <BR>  I live in Calgary AB. I have owned my 1985 V6 for two years now. It handles better than average with allseason radials. If I had winter tires, My guess would be a hole lot more control. One problem though, I have been high centered once each year. Overall I would recomend this as a great winter car, But make sure you remeber to change your antifreeze mixture. Right now I am fighting trying to unthaw my coolant lines. $#@$#^* Need I say more...?<BR>Cheers!<BR>Chris <P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||12
